{"name":"Dead Nettle White","hex":"#d2dad0","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dead","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["No longer living; deceased.","Devoid of living things; barren."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Exactly.","Very, absolutely, extremely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dead"},{"term":"nettle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant whose foliage is covered with stinging, mildly poisonous hairs, causing an instant rash.","Certain plants that have spines or prickles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of the nettle plant and similar physical causes, to sting, causing a rash in someone.","To pique, irritate, vex or provoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nettle"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lamium","blocks":[{"text":"Lamium (dead-nettles) is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Lamiaceae, of which it is the type genus. They are all herbaceous plants native to Eurasia and northern Africa, with several widely naturalised across much of the temperate world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Lamium_purpureum_-_verev_imin%C3%B5ges_Keilas.jpg/330px-Lamium_purpureum_-_verev_imin%C3%B5ges_Keilas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamium","wikidata":"Q146675"},{"title":"List of plants known as nettle","blocks":[{"text":"Nettle refers to plants with stinging hairs, particularly those of the genus Urtica. It can also refer to plants which resemble Urtica species in appearance but do not have stinging hairs, such as dead nettle or false nettle listed below. Plants called \"nettle\" include:"},{"items":["Ball nettle – Solanum carolinense","Bull nettle\nCnidoscolus stimulosus, bull nettle, spurge nettle\nCnidoscolus texanus, Texas bull nettle\nCnidoscolus urens, bull nettle\nSolanum elaeagnifolium, bull nettle, silver-leaf nettle, white horse-nettle","Cnidoscolus stimulosus, bull nettle, spurge nettle","Cnidoscolus texanus, Texas bull nettle","Cnidoscolus urens, bull nettle","Solanum elaeagnifolium, bull nettle, silver-leaf nettle, white horse-nettle","Dead nettle, dumb nettle\nLamium, particularly Lamium album","Lamium, particularly Lamium album","False nettle – Boehmeria","Flame nettle – Coleus","Hedge nettle – Stachys","Hemp nettle – Galeopsis","Horse nettle:\nAgastache urticifolia – horse-nettle\nSolanum carolinense – ball-nettle, Carolina horse-nettle\nSolanum dimidiatum – western horse-nettle, robust horse-nettle\nSolanum elaeagnifolium – bull nettle, silver-leaf nettle, white horse-nettle\nSolanum rostratum – horse-nettle","Agastache urticifolia – horse-nettle","Solanum carolinense – ball-nettle, Carolina horse-nettle","Solanum dimidiatum – western horse-nettle, robust horse-nettle","Solanum elaeagnifolium – bull nettle, silver-leaf nettle, white horse-nettle","Solanum rostratum – horse-nettle","nettle tree or tree nettle:\nCeltis\nVarious species of the genus Dendrocnide\nUrera baccifera\nUrtica ferox","Celtis","Various species of the genus Dendrocnide","Urera baccifera","Urtica ferox","Nilgiri nettle, Himalayan giant nettle - Girardinia diversifolia","Painted nettle – Coleus scutellarioides","Rock nettle – Eucnide","Shiny-leaved nettle – Dendrocnide photiniphylla","Spurge nettle – Cnidoscolus","Stinging nettle\nHesperocnide\nmost, but not all subspecies of Urtica dioica\nUrtica gracilis\nUrtica incisa\nUrtica ferox","Hesperocnide","most, but not all subspecies of Urtica dioica","Urtica gracilis","Urtica incisa","Urtica ferox","Tree nettle - see nettle tree","White nettle\nLamium album\nPipturus argenteus","Lamium album","Pipturus argenteus","Wood nettle - Laportea canadensis"]},{"items":["Cnidoscolus stimulosus, bull nettle, spurge nettle","Cnidoscolus texanus, Texas bull nettle","Cnidoscolus urens, bull nettle","Solanum elaeagnifolium, bull nettle, silver-leaf nettle, white horse-nettle"]},{"items":["Lamium, particularly Lamium album"]},{"items":["Agastache urticifolia – horse-nettle","Solanum carolinense – ball-nettle, Carolina horse-nettle","Solanum dimidiatum – western horse-nettle, robust horse-nettle","Solanum elaeagnifolium – bull nettle, silver-leaf nettle, white horse-nettle","Solanum rostratum – horse-nettle"]},{"items":["Celtis","Various species of the genus Dendrocnide","Urera baccifera","Urtica ferox"]},{"items":["Hesperocnide","most, but not all subspecies of Urtica dioica","Urtica gracilis","Urtica incisa","Urtica ferox"]},{"items":["Lamium album","Pipturus argenteus"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_plants_known_as_nettle","wikidata":"Q16872290"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Lamium_purpureum_-_v